From cf93fc172f48f8069f1d684322af786ee6e9f93c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Dawid=20Wr=C3=B3bel?= <me@dawidwrobel.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 02:01:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 03/41] tee: add Qualcomm QSEECOM TEE driver

Expose Qualcomm's QSEE trusted applications through the TEE subsystem, so an
ordinary userspace client can open a session against one and invoke commands on
it with TEE_IOC_OPEN_SESSION and TEE_IOC_INVOKE. The implementation reports
TEE_IMPL_ID_QSEECOM.

A session is opened by application *name* rather than by UUID, because that is
what QSEE matches on. Requests carry a 128-byte header and a separate payload
buffer, and the header has to contain the payload's physical address: a
VALUE_INPUT parameter says "patch the address of parameter N into the request at
this offset", so user space never has to know or supply a physical address.

Buffers TZ reads are copied through kernel-only memory rather than being mapped
to user space, so a client cannot change them while the secure world is looking
at them.

Listener requests are served by a supplicant over TEE_IOC_SUPPL_RECV and
TEE_IOC_SUPPL_SEND. There is one supplicant queue for the device, and each
request reports which listener it belongs to in arg.func, so a single supplicant
can serve several listeners. Answering a request without having filled its
buffer has been observed to hang the secure world until the watchdog fires, so
anything other than an explicit success is reported to TZ as a failure.

The supplicant is torn down when its device file is closed rather than when the
context is released: a listener holds a reference to the buffer it registered,
and tee_shm holds a reference to the context that allocated it, so releasing
only on ->release() cannot break the cycle and strands the module in "going"
state that only a reboot clears.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
